WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.387
There are two ways a device
gets an IPv6 address.

2
00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:06.888
In the first, a DHCPv6 server assigns it.

3
00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:08.617
The server knows the address,

4
00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:12.076
so it can register an AAAA
record using option thirty-nine.

5
00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:16.326
In the second, called SLAAC,
stateless address autoconfiguration,

6
00:00:16.326 --> 00:00:20.500
the device builds its own address
from what the router announces.

7
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.485
Devices also create temporary, or privacy,

8
00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:27.237
addresses, typically used
for outgoing connections.

9
00:00:27.237 --> 00:00:32.926
The DHCPv6 name option covers
only addresses DHCPv6 assigned.

10
00:00:32.926 --> 00:00:37.740
So, as an inference, no DHCPv6
server ever learns a SLAAC address,

11
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.462
and no server-side registration
creates an AAAA record for it.

12
00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:45.898
The device could register
it itself, but as we saw,

13
00:00:45.898 --> 00:00:49.162
a post-AD Windows client
can't do that securely.

14
00:00:49.162 --> 00:00:53.683
The consequence, also an inference:
for Entra-joined endpoints,

15
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:58.725
treat AAAA records for SLAAC or
privacy addresses as absent by design.

16
00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:02.781
Find endpoints by their
DHCP-assigned addresses,

17
00:01:02.781 --> 00:01:08.025
and correlate IPv6 activity from
DHCP and neighbor logs, not DNS.

18
00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:14.377
Two cloud notes: in AWS, only the instance
resource name returns AAAA records,

19
00:01:14.377 --> 00:01:20.225
and Azure's DNS Private Resolver
doesn't support IPv6-enabled subnets.

20
00:01:20.225 --> 00:01:24.300
And I couldn't verify whether Windows
registers its temporary addresses.

21
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:31.133
Sources: RFC 4862 IPv6 Stateless
Address Autoconfiguration [S37];

22
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:35.688
RFC 8981 Temporary
Address Extensions [S38];

23
00:01:35.688 --> 00:01:43.912
RFC 4704 DHCPv6 Client FQDN option [S34];
Amazon EC2 instance hostname types [S21];

24
00:01:43.912 --> 00:01:52.137
the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 Ch07 (Azure DNS Private Resolver

25
00:01:52.137 --> 00:01:56.186
platform constraints); research brief,

26
00:01:56.186 --> 00:02:01.560
Couldn't verify (Windows
temporary IPv6 addresses).
